Absolutely. Since you’re operating as the Oracle RDS DBA, I would make the exercise look like a real DBA-led disaster-recovery test rather than just an AWS failover demo.
One important check before you start: make sure your RDS deployment is the traditional Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, not a Multi-AZ DB cluster. The failover procedure differs. AWS documents that traditional Multi-AZ DB instances support Reboot with failover, while Multi-AZ DB clusters use Failover at the cluster level. �
AWS Documentation +1
Also, do this only under the approved ISCP/change window. AWS warns that reboot-with-failover abruptly interrupts the database and client sessions, so transactions should be stopped or controlled before the test. �
AWS Documentation
